<h2> Low Fidelity as Simplicity </h2>

<pre>
<p>
    Low Fidelity thrives off a limited tool kit. Less fonts, smaller file sizes, 
a single pencil and some white paper, whatever is on hand. This simplicity is 
constraining but it is also liberating. The limitation of choice allows for a 
fuller exploration of the tools on hand. Limitations encourage creativity and 
novel problem solving. Embracing the simplicity of Low Fidelity opens doors, not closes them. 


Now Low Fidelity should not be confused with Minimalism. Where Minimalism strives 
for every stroke to excel, Low Fidelity allows for the simplicity to work in its 
own right and grow, whether that is something truly simple or something far more complex.
</p>
</pre>


<h2> Low Fidelity as Complexity </h2>

<pre>
<p>    In the spaces of friction, complexity arises. Changes to the composition, 
shifts in the plans, the little unexpected moments create knots in the line 
towards the end goal. Allowing Low Fidelity to do its thing creates complexities 
in the work that creates interest and novel experience. Moments for conversation 
and contemplation that hopes to engage the reader. It's worth taking advantage of 
these complexities that come out of this system of working and allow the end goal 
to be molded by it. 
</p>
</pre>






